WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.618
The vehicle must reach a commanded depth
and heading while respecting the

00:00:04.618 --> 00:00:06.175
limits of its actuators.

00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:10.410
The side view shows depth and pitch; the
top view shows heading.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.759
The study considers a nonlinear underwater
vehicle controlled using reduced,

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:14.981
linearized prediction models.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.609
A linearized model makes repeated
optimization practical, but it does not

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:25.032
capture every nonlinear effect or
coupling.

00:00:25.032 --> 00:00:29.041
In the studied baseline, this mismatch
produces persistent depth and heading

00:00:29.041 --> 00:00:29.643
errors.

00:00:29.643 --> 00:00:34.016
This is a limitation of this baseline
formulation, not of every possible

00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:34.982
predictive controller.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.650
The adaptive reference layer adjusts the
depth and heading references supplied

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.363
to model predictive control.

00:00:42.363 --> 00:00:45.676
The predictive controller computes control
inputs while incorporating state,

00:00:45.676 --> 00:00:47.389
actuator, and rate limits.

00:00:47.389 --> 00:00:51.071
Allocation converts the virtual control
channels into commands for the

00:00:51.071 --> 00:00:52.415
physical control surfaces.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.572
Each actuator has a permitted range and a
limit on how quickly its command can

00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:00.118
change.

00:01:00.118 --> 00:01:03.648
The four tail planes combine vertical and
horizontal control demands.

00:01:03.648 --> 00:01:07.294
The optimization accounts for their
allocated commands, so a feasible virtual

00:01:07.294 --> 00:01:10.161
input must also respect the physical
actuator bounds.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.342
Conventional model predictive control
usually optimizes over a finite horizon.

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:20.786
A short prediction window may end before
the vehicle reaches its target.

00:01:20.786 --> 00:01:25.003
Here, an infinite horizon represents the
full approach from the current state

00:01:25.003 --> 00:01:27.690
to the commanded final target, including
settling.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.853
Following the infinite-horizon
domain-transformation approach of Fahroo

00:01:32.853 --> 00:01:37.360
and Ross, we map physical time onto the
interval from zero to one, excluding

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:37.891
one.

00:01:37.891 --> 00:01:41.641
In our normalization, time equals tau
divided by one minus tau.

00:01:41.641 --> 00:01:45.181
The transformed optimal control problem is
still continuous in time; this

00:01:45.181 --> 00:01:47.000
mapping is not yet discretization.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.914
Next, we choose approximation degree N and
represent states and inputs with

00:01:52.914 --> 00:01:53.867
Bernstein polynomials.

00:01:53.867 --> 00:01:55.898
Their coefficients become optimization
variables.

00:01:55.898 --> 00:01:59.493
Transcribing the cost, dynamics, and
constraints produces a finite nonlinear

00:01:59.493 --> 00:02:00.086
program.

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:03.682
Solving it determines the coefficients,
and therefore the represented state

00:02:03.682 --> 00:02:04.994
and input trajectories.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.761
Bernstein basis functions are nonnegative
and sum to one.

00:02:09.761 --> 00:02:14.020
The curve therefore stays inside the
convex hull of its control points, even

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.871
at a small degree N.

00:02:15.871 --> 00:02:19.688
Coefficient bounds in a convex admissible
set bound the represented curve.

00:02:19.688 --> 00:02:21.866
Approximation accuracy remains a separate
question.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.546
At each update, the controller uses the
latest measured state to solve a new

00:02:29.546 --> 00:02:30.836
constrained prediction problem.

00:02:30.836 --> 00:02:34.369
It applies the current control input,
receives fresh measurements, and

00:02:34.369 --> 00:02:34.952
repeats.

00:02:34.952 --> 00:02:39.192
The adaptive reference layer adjusts the
targets used by this same predictive

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:40.128
control structure.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.948
The manuscript compares predictive control
alone with the same controller

00:02:45.948 --> 00:02:47.839
assisted by adaptive reference management.

00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:51.522
Model, costs, allocation, and constraints
are retained to isolate the added

00:02:51.522 --> 00:02:52.074
layer.

00:02:52.074 --> 00:02:55.087
The reported simulations reduce
steady-state depth and heading errors

00:02:55.087 --> 00:02:58.987
while respecting actuator magnitude and
rate limits in the tested cases.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.973
The reported study uses a nonlinear
simulation plant at two forward speeds,

00:03:04.973 --> 00:03:06.667
with calm-water test conditions.

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:09.819
These animations illustrate the
architecture and constraint geometry.

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:14.920
They are not a replay of the simulation
data, and they do not establish

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:16.979
performance in arbitrary wave conditions.
